---
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { docs, repositoryUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture | OrbitKV"
  description="OrbitKV core, Luminal executor, and Rust server architecture."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
    <h1>Three layers. One KV authority.</h1>
    <p>
      OrbitKV compiles attention lifetimes and owns physical KV state. A forked
      Luminal executes the resulting model graph, while a Rust server handles
      requests without observing page identities.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Architecture" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Native pipeline</h2>
      <pre><code>{`request and token intent
    -> Rust server scheduler
    -> RuntimeManifest + RuntimeSession
    -> manager-selected pages, copies, and views
    -> Luminal graph and kernels
    -> completion evidence
    -> publication, retirement, ACK, and reuse`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Ownership boundary</h2>
      <table class="evidence-table-native">
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Authority</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>OrbitKV core</td><td>Semantics, request and Prefix snapshots, physical pages, generations, COW, token disposition, retirement, and reuse</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Luminal executor</td><td>Tensor storage, graph compilation, kernel selection, streams, events, forward execution, and sampling</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rust server</td><td>Client protocols, tokenization, batching, cancellation, backpressure, and output streaming</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Direct native composition</h2>
      <p>
        The control path stays in one Rust process. The executor derives its
        immutable plan directly from <code>RuntimeManifest</code>, then consumes
        page metadata prepared by <code>RuntimeSession</code>. The active product
        has no compatibility tree, C boundary, Python runtime, upstream
        inference HTTP proxy, general plugin framework, or parallel allocator.
        A narrow async transport contract moves bytes without gaining page
        lifecycle authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Compiled token lifetimes</h2>
      <p>
        Full, Sliding, Full+Sliding, and exact Chunked attention compile to
        different address and retirement programs. Ring layouts are derived
        physical choices. Token-level placement and disposition are always part
        of manager state; relocation is used only where semantically safe and
        economically justified.
      </p>

      <h2>Safe reuse</h2>
      <p>
        The Semantic Frontier proves that future attention cannot reach old
        state. The Execution Frontier proves that submitted device work has
        finished with it. OrbitKV requires both proofs plus exact acknowledgement
        before a physical generation can return to the free pool.
      </p>

      <h2>Current boundary</h2>
      <p>
        Compiler, manager, RuntimeSession, server contracts, and executor
        metadata lowering are host-tested. One Full checkpoint has a narrow
        real-device closure and child-graph dispatch benefit, but hybrid models,
        compiler-lifecycle L5 benefit, serving throughput, capacity, production,
        and replacement remain unqualified. Compact current evidence is listed in
        the <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/README.md`}>Results Index</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Product documents</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {docs.map((item) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <span>{item.key}</span><h3>{item.name}</h3><p>{item.detail}</p>
            <a href={item.href}>Open document →</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
